faqSchema:
  - q: "What is a healthy BMI range?"
    a: "The World Health Organization defines a healthy BMI as between 18.5 and 24.9 for adults. A BMI under 18.5 is considered underweight, 25 to 29.9 is overweight, and 30 or above is classified as obese. These thresholds are general guidelines and do not account for factors like muscle mass, age, or ethnicity."
  - q: "Is BMI the same for men and women?"
    a: "The BMI formula itself is identical for men and women. However, women naturally carry a higher percentage of body fat at the same BMI than men due to differences in body composition. This means two people with the same BMI can have quite different health profiles depending on sex, age, and muscle mass."
  - q: "Can I have a normal BMI but still be unhealthy?"
    a: "Yes. BMI does not measure body fat directly. A very muscular person can have a high BMI while having low body fat and excellent health. Conversely, someone called skinny fat can have a normal BMI with high body fat and poor metabolic health. BMI is a screening tool not a diagnosis."
  - q: "How do I calculate BMI in kg and cm?"
    a: "Divide your weight in kilograms by your height in metres squared. For example a person weighing 70 kg who is 175 cm tall has a BMI of 70 divided by 1.75 squared, which equals 22.9. This calculator does the maths automatically in both metric and imperial units."
  - q: "Does BMI change with age?"
    a: "The standard BMI formula does not adjust for age in adults. However, body composition naturally shifts as we get older. Adults over 65 often carry more body fat at the same BMI compared to younger adults. Some clinicians use slightly different thresholds for older populations, but the standard WHO ranges are still the most widely used."
