<section class="how">
  <h2>How to Use This BMI Calculator</h2>
  <p>Choose your preferred unit system at the top. Enter your current weight and height then hit Calculate. Your BMI and category appear instantly on the right.</p>
  <p>The healthy weight range shown beneath your number tells you exactly what weight you would need to be at your current height to sit in the 18.5 to 24.9 zone. This gives you a concrete target rather than just a category label.</p>
  <p>Use this alongside the <a href="/calorie-calculator/">Calorie Calculator</a> and <a href="/tdee-calculator/">TDEE Calculator</a> for a complete picture of your health metrics. BMI is a useful screening number but it works best when combined with other data points.</p>
</section>

<section class="formula">
  <h2>The BMI Formula</h2>
  <p>BMI stands for body mass index. The formula divides weight by the square of height:</p>
  <p><strong>Metric:</strong> BMI = weight (kg) / height (m)²</p>
  <p><strong>Imperial:</strong> BMI = (weight (lb) / height (in)²) x 703</p>
  <p>The number was created by Belgian mathematician Adolphe Quetelet in the 1830s. It became a medical screening tool in the 1970s when Ancel Keys validated it against body fat measurements. The World Health Organization adopted the current category thresholds in 1995.</p>
  <p>The formula does not account for where fat is distributed on the body. Waist circumference is often a better predictor of metabolic disease because abdominal fat is more metabolically active than fat stored around the hips and thighs.</p>
</section>

<section class="examples">
  <h2>BMI Calculation Examples</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Person A: 70 kg, 175 cm.</strong> BMI = 70 / (1.75 x 1.75) = 22.9. Normal weight. Healthy weight range at this height: 56.7 to 76.3 kg.</li>
    <li><strong>Person B: 90 kg, 180 cm.</strong> BMI = 90 / (1.80 x 1.80) = 27.8. Overweight. To reach the top of the healthy range this person would need to weigh around 80.6 kg.</li>
    <li><strong>Person C: 155 lb, 5 ft 6 in (168 cm).</strong> BMI = (155 / (66 x 66)) x 703 = 25.0. Right on the border of normal and overweight. This illustrates why a single pound up or down can shift your category.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

