<section class="faq">
  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

  <h3>What is a healthy BMI range?</h3>
  <p>The World Health Organization defines healthy adult BMI as 18.5 to 24.9. Below 18.5 is underweight. Between 25 and 29.9 is overweight. A BMI of 30 or above falls into the obese category. These are broad population-level benchmarks and individual health depends on many more factors than this single number.</p>

  <h3>Is BMI the same for men and women?</h3>
  <p>The formula is identical. However women naturally carry a higher percentage of body fat at the same BMI than men because of biological differences in body composition. A woman with a BMI of 22 and a man with a BMI of 22 will typically have quite different body fat percentages. This is normal and expected, not a flaw in the formula.</p>

  <h3>Can I have a normal BMI but still be unhealthy?</h3>
  <p>Absolutely. A very muscular athlete can have a BMI of 27 with extremely low body fat and excellent cardiovascular health. Someone who is sedentary could have a BMI of 23 while carrying dangerous levels of visceral fat. BMI is a population-level screening tool. It is not designed to diagnose individual health status on its own.</p>

  <h3>How do I calculate BMI in kg and cm?</h3>
  <p>Convert your height to metres first. Then divide your weight in kilograms by your height in metres squared. A person who is 70 kg and 175 cm tall: 1.75 x 1.75 = 3.0625, then 70 / 3.0625 = 22.9. This calculator does that calculation for you the moment you enter your numbers.</p>

  <h3>Does BMI change with age?</h3>
  <p>The standard formula does not adjust for age in adults. In practice, body composition shifts significantly between your 20s and your 60s. Muscle mass decreases and body fat increases even when your weight stays the same. Some researchers suggest adjusting thresholds upward for adults over 65, but the WHO ranges remain the standard reference point in clinical settings worldwide.</p>
</section>

